        {
          "path": "_m/u/6/5/d/kalmar-castle-wp/castle-chateau-kalmar-suede-sw.jpg",
          "filename": "Castle Château Kalmar Suède Sweden - photo picture image photography (9586502122).jpg",
          "caption": "Kalmar Castle is a fortified castle in Kalmar, on the Strait of Kalmar in south-eastern Sweden.\nHistory: The castle was first built by King Magnus Ladulås. It was later upgraded to the time of the Vasa by Eric XIV and his brother John III who made the castle a medieval palace fit for a king at the time of the Renaissance.\nThis castle played an important role in the history of Sweden. Thus, it is here that in 1397 the treaty of the three Scandinavian kingdoms (Denmark, Norway, Sweden) establishing the Kalmar Union under one ruler was signed: Eric of Pomerania, nephew of Queen Marguerite Ire of Denmark at the time was the real ruler of the Scandinavian countries.\n\nArchitecture: restoration of the castle in the nineteenth century. In 1856, the architect Fredrik Wilhelm Scholander (1816-1881) began restoration work in Kalmar, pursued by his pupil Helgo Zettervall (1885-1891): this work gives the castle its current form. In 1919, Professor Martin Olsson is responsible for the result of restoration work (earthworks, moats and drawbridges Bridge) in 1941, the castle was again surrounded by water. The castle is now under protection and is accessible to the public.",
          "alt": "Kalmar Castle is a fortified castle in Kalmar, on the Strait of Kalmar in south-eastern Sweden.\nHistory: The castle was first built by King Magnus Ladulås. It was later upgraded to the time of the Vasa by Eric XIV and his brother John III who made the castle a medieval palace fit for a king at the time of the Renaissance.\nThis castle played an important role in the history of Sweden. Thus, it is here that in 1397 the treaty of the three Scandinavian kingdoms (Denmark, Norway, Sweden) establishing the Kalmar Union under one ruler was signed: Eric of Pomerania, nephew of Queen Marguerite Ire of Denmark at the time was the real ruler of the Scandinavian countries.\n\nArchitecture: restoration of the castle in the nineteenth century. In 1856, the architect Fredrik Wilhelm Scholander (1816-1881) began restoration work in Kalmar, pursued by his pupil Helgo Zettervall (1885-1891): this work gives the castle its current form. In 1919, Professor Martin Olsson is responsible for the result of restoration work (earthworks, moats and drawbridges Bridge) in 1941, the castle was again surrounded by water. The castle is now under protection and is accessible to the public.",
          "width": 5616,
          "height": 3744,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY 2.0",
          "artist": "Grand Parc - Bordeaux, France from France",
